eID>\` — WRITE: rewrite the last completed compaction part's tail_start_id to the target id (the host's own lever, compaction.ts:461-466) so the retained tail shrinks/grows to it; the context re-derives from the DB at the next step — zero restart. \`tail <session> keepMessages=N\` — the keep-COUNT form (WRITE, same single transaction): N = the retained-tail MESSAGE count — the N-th message strictly before the compaction user row becomes the new tail head (the compact_memory keepMessages count semantics — a message COUNT, NOT a token budget); mutually exclusive with the <messageID> form. The core returns it as a \`tail-set= <old> -> <new> keep=N\` line of the same shape as the messageID form; it is primarily a SHRINK lever (the post-compaction trim) — growing stays available, but re-reads raw messages you no longer remember (nothing is lost — the DB keeps them, the report restores the knowledge); keep=0 stays rejected by the floor 6. Fail-closed validation mirrors the host's window guard exactly: the session exists, the last completed compaction exists (marker + summary child), the target id exists in the session, the target sits strictly BEFORE the compaction user row in (time_created, id) order, and the retained-tail count (target → compaction row, exclusive) is >= 6 — the keepMessages floor (a retained tail below 6 is rejected; the keep form's computed boundary re-runs these same checks, plus its own keep-invalid and keep-exceeds-history rejections). Single transaction; WAL — the host reads on its own connection (concurrent read safe); SQLITE_BUSY → one short-backoff retry → fail-closed (never a partial write). Backend chain: in-process sqlite (bun:sqlite → node:sqlite) first, last resort the maintainer-placed sqlite3 CLI (one CLI invocation per query; the tail transaction is ONE invocation) — a missing CLI, spawn error, or timeout still lands the fail-closed db-error. Rejections return as plain lines — \`report= rejected: <cause>\` / \`tail= rejected: <cause>\` / \`tail-set= rejected: <cause>\` (a valid fail-closed outcome, NOT a malfunction — do not retry); genuine backend failures return \`db-error:\` / \`error:\` lines. The (2) rows include the CURRENT in-flight message (its token mass is not yet committed — typically 0, not a zero-mass message); row fields: \`time\` = an epoch-ms number, mass label \`tokens=\` (provider-true) or \`bytes4=\` (bytes/4 fallback), a read target shows filePath (+ offset/limit only when set). The (3) dry-run shares the tail call's fail-closed validation — on a rejected rewrite the dry-run returns the rejection in place of the counts. Returns \`tail= <old> -> <new> keep=<count>\`, or for the keep form \`tail-set= <old> -> <new> keep=<count>\`, or the rejection reason. \`report\` never writes the DB.`,

